    <h2 id="layer1">Layer 1, part by part</h2>
    <p>
      Section 8 gives Layer 1 five parts. The shrinking quorum is built. The health registry is partly built. The other
      three are not started.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Part</th>
            <th>Status</th>
            <th>What exists</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="hl">
            <td>Shrinking quorum</td>
            <td class="m">Deployed</td>
            <td>
              <code>quorum::mode</code> and <code>quorum::decide</code>: vote on three, compare on two, self-check on
              one. <code>no_std</code>, no allocation, builds for ARM Cortex-M. The F&#180; component{' '}
              <code>RedundancyManager</code> runs it through a C interface that Kani proves equal to the core. The
              quorum demo ran it on the OPS-SAT SEPP, a Cortex-A9 flight processor, on the FlatSat and in orbit: three
              software replicas on one processor, faults from a schedule. <code>RedundancyManager</code> has not run
              there.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Node health registry</td>
            <td class="m">Deployed, partial</td>
            <td>
              <code>quorum::Health</code> counts strikes per replica. Three strikes within 200 ticks start a
              known-answer test, which retires or clears the replica. The F&#180; component <code>HealthRegistry</code>{' '}
              holds the record. The whitepaper&#39;s score of error rate, response time and gate use is not built.{' '}
              <code>quorum::Health</code> ran in the OPS-SAT runs and retired the stuck replica at tick 122; the F&#180;{' '}
              <code>HealthRegistry</code> did not run.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Memory scrubbing</td>
            <td class="m">Paper</td>
            <td>
              Listed as the F&#180; <code>ScrubScheduler</code>, not started. The demo&#39;s radiation sensor checks a
              memory block for bit flips. It counts upsets. It does not scrub.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Hardware inventory and cannibalization</td>
            <td class="m">Paper</td>
            <td>
              Not started. <code>RedundancyManager</code> can admit a spare into a replica slot after a known-answer
              test; choosing when is the inventory&#39;s job. Dusk models salvaged spares in simulation only.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Power accountant</td>
            <td class="m">Paper</td>
            <td>
              Listed as the F&#180; <code>PowerAccountant</code>, not started. <code>RedundancyManager</code> can put a
              replica on standby, the hook it will use.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">
      F&#180; component status from{' '}
      <a href="https://github.com/mruspace/flight/blob/main/docs/fprime-design.md">docs/fprime-design.md</a> in
      mruspace/flight.
    </p>

    <h2 id="shared">Shared parts</h2>
    <p>These parts serve every layer.</p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Part</th>
            <th>Whitepaper</th>
            <th>Status</th>
            <th>What exists</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Duty-cycle scheduler</td>
            <td class="m">§4.5</td>
            <td class="m">Paper</td>
            <td>None. The whitepaper does not yet assign it to a layer.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Memory tiers</td>
            <td class="m">§4.3</td>
            <td class="m">Paper</td>
            <td>None.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Degradation model and mission phases</td>
            <td class="m">§13</td>
            <td class="m">Paper</td>
            <td>None. Dusk models processor loss, not the power phases.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hl">
            <td>Simulation framework</td>
            <td class="m">§14</td>
            <td class="m">Prototype, partial</td>
            <td>
              <a href="/simulation/dusk/">Dusk</a> models processor failures, correlated failures, salvaged spares and
              upsets. It does not model power decay, thermal limits, memory decay or science targets.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      Section 14 names six metrics. Dusk reports useful work, wrong results and the day output ends, which is survival
      duration. The other five need power and science models that Dusk does not have yet. Dusk&#39;s parameters are
      illustrative, not calibrated. The <a href="/core/bench/">bench</a> measures useful work, survival and the Graceful
      Degradation Index on real processes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="products">Products and research</h2>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Mru Flight</strong> is Layer 1 for spacecraft. The F&#180; components <code>RedundancyManager</code>,{' '}
        <code>HealthRegistry</code> and <code>ReplicaHost</code> are built and tested: 37 unit tests, and a system test
        against the demo&#39;s counts on the bench schedules. They have not run on flight hardware. The quorum demo,
        without F&#180;, <a href="/core/quorum-cli/#opssat">ran in orbit on ESA&#39;s OPS-SAT PRETTY satellite</a> on 18
        Oct 2026 (exp4051), as three software replicas on one Linux payload processor with faults from a schedule.
        Results matched the prediction written before the run.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mru Field</strong> is the same Layer 1 core for systems on Earth that are hard to reach, as a supervisor
        that runs an operator&#39;s task as three replicas on one processor, acquires every input once and resumes from
        a checkpoint after a power cut: version 0.1.0 (21 Oct 2026) in{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/mruspace/field">mruspace/field</a>, tested on the bench on general-purpose
        processors, not on a board in a system yet. See <a href="https://mru.space/field/">Mru Field</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dusk</strong> is the first part of the §14 simulation framework.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mru 2049</strong> is not part of the layers. It is a <a href="/lab/2049/">browser flight simulator</a>{' '}
        on real astronomical data.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="next">What comes next</h2>
    <p>In order:</p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Run the full bench on target-class processors.</strong> The quorum demo has run once on a flight
        processor, on the FlatSat and in orbit; the 300-run <a href="/core/bench/">bench</a> with reboots and power cuts
        has not.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Calibrate Dusk</strong> with radiation or flight data. Its policy model is already{' '}
        <a href="/core/bench/#model">checked against the bench</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Specify Layer 0.</strong> The instruction set, the format of behavior programs, the integrity check
        against Tier 0, and the properties to prove. Code starts after the specification.
      </li>
    </ul>

